// `#no_abc` and the bounds-check build setting (ADR-0058) — the second feature of W3.
//
// ADR-0003 decided in the *vertical slice* that bounds checking is a build setting carried as an
// explicit MIR operation a pass strips, with a local opt-out. The operation landed with arrays
// (ADR-0039); the pass and the flag never did, and PLAN.md §1.5 said "the operation exists and the
// pass that strips it does not" for eleven waves. Both exist now.
//
// **This file cannot observe the interesting thing, and that is worth stating.** A stripped bounds
// check is invisible in any program that stays in range — and a corpus file must check and run
// cleanly, so every index here is in range. Reading `buf[9]` with the checks off would read whatever
// is at that address, which is undefined behaviour by construction: that is what the flag buys, and
// a test asserting what it produces would be asserting a fact about this machine's stack.
//
// So the evidence for the pass lives in three other places (ADR-0058 §5):
//
//   * `crates/jr-db/tests/optimized_mir.rs` counts `BoundsCheck` statements under each setting,
//     which is the only *direct* evidence the pass ran;
//   * `differential.rs` runs this file in both engines, which is what says a valid program computes
//     the same answer either way — a build setting that changed an answer would be a miscompile;
//   * a test asserting the check is still there by default, because a pass that always stripped
//     would satisfy everything else.
//
// What this file *does* prove is the half that is observable: that `#no_abc` parses, formats,
// checks, lowers and runs — in a procedure that indexes, alongside one that does not have the
// directive, so the two lower differently from the same source.
//
// The `exit` is what gives it teeth under the differential harness: only two corpus programs print,
// so a computation has to be made observable through an exit status.
#import "Basic";

/// Indexes with the check suppressed (ADR-0058 §3). The index comes from a **parameter**, so
/// nothing in the mid-end can prove it in range — which is what makes this procedure's MIR
/// genuinely different from `safe`'s below rather than identical after const-prop deleted both.
read_fast :: (buf: [4]s64, i: s64) -> s64 #no_abc {
    return buf[i];
}

/// The same body without the directive, so its check survives. Here to make the difference between
/// the two a property of the *declaration* rather than of the build — a file-level reading of
/// `#no_abc` would give these two the same MIR.
read_safe :: (buf: [4]s64, i: s64) -> s64 {
    return buf[i];
}

/// A `for` over an array also emits a check per element, at a second site in the lowering path
/// (ADR-0049). `#no_abc` has to reach that one too, or the directive would work on `buf[i]` and
/// silently not on a loop — two emission sites is how they come to disagree.
sum_fast :: (buf: [4]s64) -> s64 #no_abc {
    total := 0;
    for x: buf {
        total = total + x;
    }
    return total;
}

/// Both attributes on one procedure, in the order the parser accepts second. `#c_call` and
/// `#no_abc` are independent — one is about the calling convention and the other about indexing —
/// and writing both must be legal in either order, because an ordering rule is one no reader could
/// guess. This also pins the formatter against reordering them.
raw_fast :: (buf: [4]s64, i: s64) -> s64 #no_abc #c_call {
    return buf[i];
}

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    xs: [4]s64;
    xs[0] = 1;
    xs[1] = 2;
    xs[2] = 4;
    xs[3] = 8;

    // The unchecked read and the checked one must agree, in range. This is the whole safety
    // argument for the flag: removing a check cannot change a valid program's answer.
    if read_fast(xs, 2) == 4 {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    if read_safe(xs, 2) == 4 {
        n = n + 2;
    }
    if read_fast(xs, 2) == read_safe(xs, 2) {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // Every element, through the `for` lowering's own check site.
    if sum_fast(xs) == 15 {
        n = n + 8;
    }

    // `#no_abc` and `#c_call` together.
    if raw_fast(xs, 3) == 8 {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    // The first and last elements, which are the two a wrong bound is most likely to catch.
    if read_fast(xs, 0) == 1 {
        n = n + 32;
    }
    if read_fast(xs, 3) == 8 {
        n = n + 64;
    }

    // Every assertion: 127.
    if n == 127 {
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}
